    "result_text": "The full test suite is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers in `TelephoneNormalizer.java`.\n\n**Change** (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/TelephoneNormalizer.java`):\n\n- Added a `NATIONAL_DIGITS_BY_COUNTRY_CODE` map declaring the required national-significant digit count per recognised country calling code: `+61` \u2192 9, `+1` \u2192 10.\n- Added a `hasValidNationalLength(...)` helper that strips the leading `+`, matches the canonical number's leading country code, and verifies the remaining national digits have exactly the required length. Codes not in the map pass through (still bound only by the generic 8\u201315 digit range), preserving existing behavior.\n- Wired that check into `normalize(...)` alongside the existing bounds check, so a wrong national length is rejected via `InvalidOwnerFieldsException`, which `ExceptionControllerAdvice` renders as a **400** with `telephone` in the `errors` array.\n\nThe duplicate-detection path (`canonicalize`) is untouched, so existing seed data used only for comparison is unaffected.\n\nThe new acceptance test `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (posting `+61 123` \u2192 400) passes, and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures).",
